# Individual Reflection – Holiday Maker

## Work and Implementation

When I started this course about three weeks ago, I had no previous experience with databases or MySQL. I had to learn the basics while also working on the project, which was sometimes challenging.

At first, I found it difficult to understand how databases worked and how all the different parts fit together. Linus introduced the comparison between databases and Excel, where a schema is roughly like an Excel file, a table is like a sheet, and columns and rows work in a similar way. I kept this comparison in mind throughout the course because it helped me understand how information is organised and filtered.

During the course, I practised using SQL to retrieve, filter, sort, add, update, and delete information. I also learned how JOIN can be used to combine information from several tables. The book *Studera SQL – prata med data* by Benjamin Berglund helped me understand relational databases, data types, keys, relationships, and data modelling. I found it useful to connect the explanations in the book to the practical work in my project.

I also worked with Homenet, a ready-made database that we downloaded as a ZIP file and used in MySQL. Through the exercises, I practised SQL queries and learned how to use Docker to run MySQL.

At the beginning of the project, I created an ER diagram together with my group. When I worked on my individual solution, I made several changes. I corrected the relationship between Hotels and Rooms so that a hotel can have zero or more rooms, while each room belongs to exactly one hotel. I also renamed distance_to_city to distance_to_center to make the name clearer.

I removed is_booked and is_cancelled from the Bookings table. I considered is_booked unnecessary because each row already represents a booking. I removed is_cancelled because my program cancels a booking by deleting the booking row. I also removed the separate price table and stored the price directly in Rooms instead.

One thing that took me time to understand was foreign keys and how they connect tables. I gradually understood that a foreign key refers to a primary key in another table. For example, the Rooms table contains a hotel ID that refers to the correct hotel in the Hotels table. Seeing how this worked in my own database made it easier to understand why these connections are important.

I also learned about UNIQUE constraints. In my database, customer email addresses must be unique, so the same email address cannot be registered more than once. I also used a UNIQUE constraint for the combination of hotel ID and room number. This means that a hotel cannot have two rooms with the same room number, while different hotels can still use the same room number.

My database also uses a junction table between Hotels and Amenities. A hotel can have several amenities, and the same amenity can be available at several hotels. The junction table connects these tables without storing several amenities directly in Hotels. It also means that new amenities can be added without changing the structure of the Hotels table. This helped me understand many-to-many relationships and why information is separated into different tables.

I used SQL files to create the tables and insert test data. I then used DataGrip to check the tables and their contents. I also took part in a seminar presentation where I could compare my solution with other groups. One group had chosen to use four tables, while my database had more. This showed me that databases can be organised in different ways depending on how the information is structured.

I used DataGrip to work with the database, Docker to run MySQL, and MySqlConnector to connect my C# program to the database. I also used VS Code for the C# code, PowerShell for commands, and Git for version control. Gradually, I understood how these tools work together and what each one is used for.

## Results

The result was a console application where users can register or select a customer and search for available hotel rooms. They can search by location, dates, and number of people, filter by amenities and distance, and sort results by price or hotel rating.

The program uses SQL queries and JOIN to retrieve information from several tables, such as hotels, rooms, capacity, price, and bookings. Users can also make bookings, view existing bookings, change the number of people, and cancel bookings.

The program calculates the total price by multiplying the price per night by the number of nights. I also worked with the booking dates so that the checkout date does not count as a booked night. This means that a new booking can start on the same day that another booking ends.

The program also contains a check for overlapping bookings. It checks whether a room is already booked during the selected period before saving a new booking. Understanding this logic helped me see why availability needs to be checked as part of the booking process.

I think the result turned out well, considering that databases were completely new to me when the course started. The main features are in place, although there are still things I would like to improve.

## Tests and Checks

I tested the database connection, searches, filters, and sorting options. I also tested changing the number of people in a booking. Changing the number from two to one worked, while trying to enter three people in a room with a capacity of two was rejected.

I also tested cancelling a booking and checked that it no longer appeared in the customer's bookings. After restarting the program, the other bookings were still saved. I also used dotnet build to check that the project compiled without errors.

I used Copilot for support when I needed to understand the booking logic and how the program checks for overlapping dates.

In DataGrip, I also ran a separate SQL query to find bookings for the same room with overlapping dates. The query found five pairs of overlapping bookings in the existing test data. This showed me how SQL can be used to check the database and find problems in the stored information.

Through this work, I learned that it is important to check both that the program works as expected and that the database contains correct information.

## Risks and Limitations

One risk in a booking system is that the same room could be booked by several customers for overlapping dates. That is why the program checks availability before saving a booking. Another important check is room capacity, so that a room cannot be booked for more people than it can hold.

The program uses parameterized SQL queries, where user input is sent separately from the SQL command. This helps reduce the risk of SQL injection.

One limitation is that the program is a console application without a graphical interface. It is also designed for one operator at a time and does not have a login system or different user permissions.

If I had had more time, I would have liked to develop access control and authentication further. Benjamin introduced us to these topics during the course, and I think they would be useful for making the system more secure.

Another limitation is that the program only allows booking dates in December 2026, and the check-in date cannot be earlier than the current date.

## Improvements

If I continued working on Holiday Maker, I would like to create a graphical interface and develop login and access control so that different users could have different permissions.

I would also like to improve error handling and spend more time testing different booking situations, especially overlapping dates and availability checks.

## Use of AI and Other Support

During the project, I used ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot for support. Since databases were new to me, I often needed help understanding SQL, relationships, data types, and how the C# program communicates with the database.

I also used Copilot to help me understand parts of the code, for example, the booking logic and how overlapping dates are checked. I used the explanations as support, but I still needed to read the code, understand the suggestions, and check that the program worked as intended.

I did not want to simply copy suggestions from AI. I wanted to understand why something was needed and make sure it suited my project. I checked my work by reading the code, running SQL queries, testing the program, and building the project.

## Conclusion

When I think back to how I started the course three weeks ago, I feel that I have learned a lot in a short time. I went from having no experience with databases to creating tables, understanding relationships, using SQL, and connecting a database to a C# program.

At first, many things felt disconnected, but over time I started to understand how the database, SQL, and C# work together to retrieve, save, and update information.

I have tried to understand why the solutions work instead of just making them work. Working with the ER diagram, foreign keys, and booking logic helped me understand how database design affects the program.

There is still a lot for me to learn, but I feel that this project has given me a good foundation for future courses and projects.